r"""
CHAIN-008 - Deleting a Rocket.Chat message deletes nothing: an outsider learns
            what was deleted and when, a member reads the text back, and an
            unauthenticated caller reads it too

Steps (each proven on its own in stage 11; this script runs them as one attack):
  1. VULN-010  chat.getDeletedMessages has no authorization check, so any account
               lists the ids and timestamps of the deletions in any room by id,
               including rooms it is not a member of.
               -> the deletion timeline of the workspace, and the message ids the
                  next steps need.
  2. VULN-059  chat.syncThreadsList returns the hidden full copy of a deleted
               thread root, on a default install, to any member of the room
               (deleteMessage keeps a copy whenever the message has replies,
               whatever the history setting says).
               -> the deleted text itself, with no non-default setting at all.
  3. VULN-049  with Message_KeepHistory on, every delete and edit marks the
               message `_hidden` instead of removing it, and chat.getMessage
               returns it by id with no hidden filter.
               -> any deleted or edited-away message of a room the caller can
                  read, not just thread roots. (Note what the run shows about the
                  two configurations: with history keeping on nothing is moved to
                  the trash collection, so step 1's timeline is empty there, and
                  with it off the timeline is complete but only thread roots keep
                  their text. The two legs widen the same hole from opposite
                  ends; the attacker does not need both at once.)
  4. VULN-047  with anonymous read on, channels.anonymousread returns a channel's
               messages with no hidden-message filter and no account at all.
               -> the same deleted content to an unauthenticated caller: the
                  boundary the chain crosses is from "the room's members" to
                  "anybody who can reach the workspace".

Vulnerable code:
  targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/api/v1/chat.ts (getDeletedMessages: no
      room access check; getMessage and syncThreadsList: no `_hidden` filter)
  targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/lib/messages/deleteMessage.ts:32-33
      (a thread root is always kept as a hidden copy)
  targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/api/v1/channels.ts:2116-2122
      (anonymousread returns messages without the hidden filter)

The chain
---------
The product's own UI promises a deletion: the message becomes "message removed"
and the room's history, search and thread list all stop showing it. Each finding
undoes part of that promise, and the pieces fit: the ids and times come from an
account with no relationship to the room, the content comes back by id, and the
last step drops even the requirement to have an account. The result is that
"delete" is a display preference, and anything a user deleted because it should
not have been said (a pasted credential, a customer's details, a sentence about a
colleague) is still readable, in the worst case by anybody on the network.

Two of the four steps need a business feature an administrator chose: keeping
message edit history (Message_KeepHistory) and anonymous read
(Accounts_AllowAnonymousRead). The run turns each on through the workspace's own
settings API and restores it before it exits. Step 2 needs neither: it is the
shipped configuration.

What a successful run proves
---------------------------
An account that is not a member of a private group, and cannot open it at all,
listed that room's deletions with their ids and times; a member recovered the
full text of a deleted thread root on the shipped configuration; with history
keeping on, a member recovered the text of an ordinary deleted message by id; and
with anonymous read on, an unauthenticated request returned both of those deleted
texts.

Dependencies: requests.
"""
import datetime as _dt
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re as _re
import secrets
import sys
import time

import requests

# --- Blue Spider standalone exploit preamble (self-contained; copy, do not import) ---
